---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="PT-141 (Bremelanotide): FDA-Approved Use — Nawtch"
  description="Vyleesi's FDA-approved use for HSDD, how it works, standard prescribing info, and side effects — not medical advice."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/pt-141/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>PT-141 (Bremelanotide)</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">Vyleesi — approved specifically for low sexual desire in premenopausal women.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent2); background:rgba(96,200,240,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent3);">Tier 1 — FDA-Approved Prescription Medication</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Bremelanotide is a real, regulated drug with clinical trial data behind its one
            approved use, available only by prescription. This page describes that approved use
            and standard prescribing information — that's public medical information about a
            regulated drug, not personalized medical advice. <strong>Talk to your doctor</strong> about whether it's appropriate for you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            "PT-141" is the research code name for bremelanotide, a synthetic peptide that
            activates melanocortin receptors (mainly MC4R) in the brain rather than acting on sex
            hormones or blood flow directly — a different mechanism from erectile-dysfunction
            drugs like sildenafil, which work on blood vessels. Activating this brain pathway is
            the proposed basis for its effect on sexual desire.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">FDA-Approved Use</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Approved in 2019 as <strong>Vyleesi</strong>, specifically for acquired, generalized
            hypoactive sexual desire disorder (HSDD) in premenopausal women — low sexual desire
            that causes marked distress and isn't better explained by another medical, relationship,
            or psychiatric issue, or by medication side effects. It is not approved for men, for
            postmenopausal women, or for any use outside this specific diagnosis.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2019/210557s000lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Vyleesi Prescribing Information →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Standard Prescribing Information</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Vyleesi is self-administered via a prefilled autoinjector into the abdomen or thigh,
            at least 45 minutes before anticipated sexual activity, as needed rather than on a
            daily schedule. Labeling advises no more than one dose within 24 hours and no more
            than 8 doses per month. This is general public prescribing information, not a
            personalized recommendation — your prescriber determines whether and how to use it in
            your case.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Side Effects &amp; Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Each dose can cause a temporary rise in blood pressure and drop in heart rate,
            typically resolving within about 12 hours — a reason it's used cautiously, and
            generally avoided, in people with uncontrolled high blood pressure or known
            cardiovascular disease. Nausea is common, especially with the first doses, along with
            flushing and headache. It can also temporarily darken skin and gums with frequent,
            long-term use.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2019/210557s000lbl.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Vyleesi (Bremelanotide) Prescribing Information</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
